--- a/USAGE.md
+++ b/USAGE.md
@@ -31,6 +31,14 @@ physical-button update, and expiry checks. Run these on Linux/Pi before deployin
 
 ## Start and connect
 
+### Physical motion telemetry (UPS2/UPT2)
+
+Send the same subscription with magic `UPS2` to request an 88-byte `UPT2` reply. Its first 56 bytes match UPT1 except for the magic. The extension is `generation:u64be | snapshot_monotonic_ns:u64be | total_physical_x:u32be | total_physical_y:u32be | last_motion_age_us:u32be | reserved:u32be=0`.
+
+X/Y totals wrap modulo 2^32 and include only matching physical reports, never UPX1 or ASCII-injected movement. A generation change invalidates the receiver's baseline. Age saturates at 0xffffffff, also used if no nonzero motion has been observed. Counts and timestamp are sampled under the registry lock; the UDP worker sends snapshots at the existing heartbeat rate. UPS1 clients still receive the original 56-byte UPT1 reply. This extension is required only for direction-based assistance.
+
+The codec test covers both versions. The UDP integration test also checks that injected motion does not enter the physical counters. Validate with `make test` on Linux/Pi before deployment.
+
 Build and start the Pi proxy as shown in [README.md](README.md). UDP listens on port **12345** only with `--enable_injection`. `USB_PROXY_BIND` selects the local IPv4 address (default `0.0.0.0`). `USB_PROXY_PEER` optionally restricts control to one source IPv4 address. This is an unauthenticated LAN protocol; the IP restriction is not cryptographic authentication.
 
 Move the physical mouse once after enumeration. `+state` returns `not_ready` until a supported descriptor and matching physical report have been observed. Unsupported reports keep passing through without synthesized mouse commands.
